WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:03.400
Abraham: So, what exactly are
networks and what do they do?

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.609
Let’s look at these in more detail.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:10.639
Nicole: A LAN or Local Area Network
connects devices such as personal

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:14.920
computers and workstations that
share tools, data and programmes.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:20.340
These may be in homes, schools, hospitals
and small businesses and may use an

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.509
ethernet protocol to define how the
information moves around the network.

00:00:25.319 --> 00:00:28.200
They are connected using a
private addressing scheme as

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.109
defined by the TCP/IP protocol.

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:36.969
For increased speed and security, a LAN
relies on wired connections, however,

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:39.230
wireless connections are also used.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:45.240
LANs using wireless connections are
known as Wireless Local Area Networks.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.710
The protocol used to share data is WiFi
but they can also operate with Bluetooth.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.960
Data can transmit at a fast rate if the
number of linked computers is limited.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:01.070
They can also connect anything from
two computers to several hundreds.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.269
To connect more than a couple of
machines a hub would be used, this would

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:07.169
link to each machine in the network.

00:01:07.719 --> 00:01:12.890
Also, other devices can be linked to a
LAN such as printers for everyone to use.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.960
To connect to the internet or a WAN a
router would be needed rather than a hub.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.489
Abraham: A MAN, or Metropolitan Area
Network covers a larger area than a LAN.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.780
It can connect various
users who are situated in a

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.430
specific geographical region.

00:01:29.219 --> 00:01:33.009
It can be found within a large
University or Hospital campus, a

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.539
telephone company or cable TV network.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:39.620
It may serve as an ISP
[internet service provider].

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.170
The overall goal of a MAN is to
develop the links between LANs.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.090
It is designed for high-speed
connectivity and will use optical

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.160
fibres and switches and routers.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:54.929
Switches perform 2 key functions,
filtering data and managing connections.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:00.989
Nicole: A WAN or Wide Area Network is
a computer network that extends over

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:03.209
a large specific geographical area.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:08.590
This may be a large city or a country
or could be the size of the world

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:10.799
if the internet was one entity.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.590
A WAN connects LANs to other
LANs and to MANs using ISPs.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.390
WAN's can be public or private.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:24.159
An organisation could have a company
WAN which covers small regional offices,

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.500
the main head office and cloud services.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:31.170
Devices used to transmit data
through WAN are optical fibres,

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.010
microwaves, and satellites.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.250
WANs by the nature of their size
often have to cross territorial

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:42.680
boundaries and international borders
and as so are subject to different

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.840
legal jurisdictions which may in
turn lead to network usage rights.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.790
Often also network cables run under the
sea which can open them up to damage by

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.460
weather, marine traffic, or sabotage.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:00.280
Data sent over networks such as the
internet is divided into packets.

00:03:01.049 --> 00:03:04.430
This means large amount of
data is split up so that it

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.010
doesn't slow down the system.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.770
It is sent in small packages
through the wires, cables and

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:11.329
radio waves of the internet.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.549
The data is then reconstituted in the
correct order by the recipient’s device.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.230
It is possible theoretically to
send data over the internet without

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.930
chopping it into small packets.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:27.879
It could be sent as a line of bits,
but this approach is impractical when

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.919
more than 2 computers are involved.

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:35.150
Abraham: The main two ways in which
packets are sent are TCP and UDP.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.739
TCP ensures that all data packets
arrive where they need to, with

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.150
a handshake done to confirm.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:46.460
An example of this is web traffic – it’s
important you get all the packets so

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:48.059
that the website renders correctly.

00:03:48.059 --> 00:03:52.379
UDP does not have a handshake
and sends all packets and

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:53.720
doesn’t care if they all arrive.

00:03:54.559 --> 00:03:58.550
An example of UDP traffic is live
video streaming – you wouldn’t want the

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:02.170
whole programme you’re watching to stop
just because one packet representing

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:05.420
a tiny part of the stream didn’t come
through because of a network blip.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.130
The internet is a packet switching
network allowing packets to be

00:04:10.139 --> 00:04:11.950
processed independently from each other.

00:04:12.779 --> 00:04:16.680
As a result, billions of devices
can exchange data at the same time.

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:22.349
Network traffic is a term that refers
to packets however not all packets are

00:04:22.349 --> 00:04:24.409
good and not all network traffic is safe.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.699
Attackers can generate malicious network
traffic designed to compromise or

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.020
overwhelm a network in the form of DDoS.

